<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris Peace Accords</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Peace Accords were signed on 27 January 1973 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, South Vietnam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>'s Provisional Revolutionary Government, after years of negotiation. They provided for a ceasefire, the withdrawal of the remaining US forces and the return of prisoners of war, but left North Vietnamese troops in the south. Fighting resumed, and North Vietnam conquered the south in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords.jpg" alt="Paris Peace Accords" width="320" height="570" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sanjay Acharya · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam_Peace_Treaty_1973.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was divided at the 17th parallel in July 1954, and a war between the communist north and the south followed, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> backing the south. After the Tet Offensive of 1968 and the rise of the American anti-war movement, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a> halted bombing of the northern part of North Vietnam on 31 March 1968, and delegations first met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 10 May. Talks stalled for five months until Johnson agreed on 31 October to end the air strikes. Disputes over recognition were settled by naming North Vietnam and the United States as the parties, and one over the shape of the table by a circular table for the two governments and square tables for the other parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> became president in 1969, the main negotiations were secret talks between Henry Kissinger and Lê Đức Thọ, begun on 4 August 1969. On 8 May 1972 Nixon accepted a cease-fire in place before American withdrawal, and on 8 October 1972 Thọ allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> government to remain in power. Kissinger announced that peace was at hand. President Thiệu, who had not been told of the talks, refused the draft, so Nixon ordered the Operation Linebacker II bombings in December 1972, pledged continued aid and pressed Thiệu, who had little choice but to accede. The agreement was signed at the Hotel Majestic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 27 January 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The agreement provided for a cease-fire, the withdrawal of American and allied forces within sixty days, the return of prisoners and peaceful reunification, and set up an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-commission-of-control">International Commission of Control</a> and Supervision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. North Vietnamese troops stayed in the south. Fighting did not stop on 28 January, full-scale war had resumed by March 1973, and Thiệu declared the agreement no longer in effect on 4 January 1974. The Case-Church Amendment of June 1973 barred further American military activity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, and aid to the south was cut. North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s final offensive ended with the surrender of Dương Văn Minh on 30 April 1975. Kissinger and Thọ received the 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a>, though Thọ declined it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paris Peace Accords” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Paris Peace Accords</b>, signed twelve days later, ended direct American involvement in the war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The final American combat units departed under the <b>Paris Peace Accords</b>, and the last prisoners of war were released.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-29">29 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Paris Peace Accords</b> of 1973 and the fall of Saigon in April 1975, the DRV merged with the Republic of South Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Paris Peace Accords</b> of 1973 US forces withdrew, and North Vietnam's spring offensive of 1975 overran the south.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam</a></li></ul></section>
